[{"input":"# Lecture 8 – Computer Networks and the Internet \n\n## Learning Objectives \n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to: \n\n- Explain why computer networks exist. \n\n- Distinguish between local networks and the Internet. \n\n- Understand the concepts of protocols, packets, clients, and servers. \n\n- Explain the roles of IP, TCP, UDP, and DNS. \n\n- Understand how information travels across the Internet. \n\n- Describe how websites are accessed. \n\n- Understand the concepts of cloud computing and online services. \n\n- Follow the complete journey of a web request. ","output":"","result":"# Lecture 8 – Computer Networks and the Internet \n\n## Learning Objectives \n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to: \n\n- Explain why computer networks exist. \n\n- Distinguish between local networks and the Internet. \n\n- Understand the concepts of protocols, packets, clients, and servers. \n\n- Explain the roles of IP, TCP, UDP, and DNS. \n\n- Understand how information travels across the Internet. \n\n- Describe how websites are accessed. \n\n- Understand the concepts of cloud computing and online services. \n\n- Follow the complete journey of a web request. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3469-lecture-8-computer-networks-and-the-internet\">\n<h1>Lecture 8 – Computer Networks and the Internet</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3469-learning-objectives\">\n<h1>Learning Objectives</h1>\n<p>At the end of this chapter, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Explain why computer networks exist.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Distinguish between local networks and the Internet.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Understand the concepts of protocols, packets, clients, and servers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Explain the roles of IP, TCP, UDP, and DNS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Understand how information travels across the Internet.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Describe how websites are accessed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Understand the concepts of cloud computing and online services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Follow the complete journey of a web request.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Introduction \n\nSo far, we have studied a single computer: \n\n- Hardware \n\n- Software \n\n- Operating systems \n\nHowever, modern computing is rarely performed in isolation. \n\nMost applications depend on communication between computers. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Viewing websites \n\n- Sending emails \n\n- Streaming videos \n\n- Online gaming \n\n- Social media \n\n- Cloud storage \n\n- Artificial intelligence services \n\nThis communication is enabled by computer networks. ","output":"","result":"## Introduction \n\nSo far, we have studied a single computer: \n\n- Hardware \n\n- Software \n\n- Operating systems \n\nHowever, modern computing is rarely performed in isolation. \n\nMost applications depend on communication between computers. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Viewing websites \n\n- Sending emails \n\n- Streaming videos \n\n- Online gaming \n\n- Social media \n\n- Cloud storage \n\n- Artificial intelligence services \n\nThis communication is enabled by computer networks. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3471-introduction\">\n<h1>Introduction</h1>\n<p>So far, we have studied a single computer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Hardware</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Software</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Operating systems</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, modern computing is rarely performed in isolation.</p>\n<p>Most applications depend on communication between computers.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Viewing websites</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Sending emails</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Streaming videos</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Online gaming</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Social media</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud storage</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Artificial intelligence services</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This communication is enabled by computer networks.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## What Is a Network? \n\nA network is a collection of devices that can communicate and exchange information. \n\nExamples of devices include: \n\n- Computers \n\n- Smartphones \n\n- Printers \n\n- Servers \n\n- Routers \n\n- Smart TVs \n\n### Everyday Example \n\nAt home you may have: \n```text\n Laptop \n Smartphone \n Printer \n Smart TV\n```\n\nall connected to the same network. \n\nThis allows them to exchange information. \n\n### Benefits of Networks \n\nNetworks allow devices to: \n\n- Share information \n\n- Share resources \n\n- Communicate efficiently \n\n- Access remote services \n\nWithout networks, every computer would operate independently. ","output":"","result":"## What Is a Network? \n\nA network is a collection of devices that can communicate and exchange information. \n\nExamples of devices include: \n\n- Computers \n\n- Smartphones \n\n- Printers \n\n- Servers \n\n- Routers \n\n- Smart TVs \n\n### Everyday Example \n\nAt home you may have: \n```text\n Laptop \n Smartphone \n Printer \n Smart TV\n```\n\nall connected to the same network. \n\nThis allows them to exchange information. \n\n### Benefits of Networks \n\nNetworks allow devices to: \n\n- Share information \n\n- Share resources \n\n- Communicate efficiently \n\n- Access remote services \n\nWithout networks, every computer would operate independently. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3472-what-is-a-network\">\n<h1>What Is a Network?</h1>\n<p>A network is a collection of devices that can communicate and exchange information.</p>\n<p>Examples of devices include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Computers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Smartphones</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Printers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Servers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Routers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Smart TVs</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3472-everyday-example\">\n<h1>Everyday Example</h1>\n<p>At home you may have:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\"> Laptop \n Smartphone \n Printer \n Smart TV</pre>\n<p>all connected to the same network.</p>\n<p>This allows them to exchange information.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3472-benefits-of-networks\">\n<h1>Benefits of Networks</h1>\n<p>Networks allow devices to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Share information</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Share resources</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Communicate efficiently</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Access remote services</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without networks, every computer would operate independently.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Local Networks and the Internet \n\n### Local Area Network (LAN) \n\nA Local Area Network connects devices within a limited area. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Home network \n\n- School network \n\n- Office network \n\n \n\n#### Example \n\n```text\n     Laptop \n        │ \n     Router \n        │ \n     Printer \n```\n\nAll devices communicate locally. \n\n\n### Internet \n\nThe Internet is a global network connecting millions of smaller networks. \n\nRather than being a single computer, the Internet is: \n\n```text\n     Network \n     connected to \n     Network \n     connected to \n     Network \n     ... \n```\n\non a worldwide scale. \n\n \n\n### Important Distinction \n\n#### LAN \n\n```text\n     Small Area \n     Private\n```\n\n#### Internet \n\n```text\n     Global \n     Public \n```","output":"","result":"## Local Networks and the Internet \n\n### Local Area Network (LAN) \n\nA Local Area Network connects devices within a limited area. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Home network \n\n- School network \n\n- Office network \n\n \n\n#### Example \n\n```text\n     Laptop \n        │ \n     Router \n        │ \n     Printer \n```\n\nAll devices communicate locally. \n\n\n### Internet \n\nThe Internet is a global network connecting millions of smaller networks. \n\nRather than being a single computer, the Internet is: \n\n```text\n     Network \n     connected to \n     Network \n     connected to \n     Network \n     ... \n```\n\non a worldwide scale. \n\n \n\n### Important Distinction \n\n#### LAN \n\n```text\n     Small Area \n     Private\n```\n\n#### Internet \n\n```text\n     Global \n     Public \n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3473-local-networks-and-the-internet\">\n<h1>Local Networks and the Internet</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3473-local-area-network-lan\">\n<h1>Local Area Network (LAN)</h1>\n<p>A Local Area Network connects devices within a limited area.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Home network</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>School network</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Office network</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3473-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Laptop \n        │ \n     Router \n        │ \n     Printer </pre>\n<p>All devices communicate locally.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3473-internet\">\n<h1>Internet</h1>\n<p>The Internet is a global network connecting millions of smaller networks.</p>\n<p>Rather than being a single computer, the Internet is:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Network \n     connected to \n     Network \n     connected to \n     Network \n     ... </pre>\n<p>on a worldwide scale.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3473-important-distinction\">\n<h1>Important Distinction</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3473-lan\">\n<h1>LAN</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Small Area \n     Private</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3473-internet-2\">\n<h1>Internet</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Global \n     Public </pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Communication Requires Rules \n\nImagine a conversation where everyone uses different languages. \n\nCommunication would be difficult. \n\nComputers face the same challenge. \n\nThey need common rules. \n\n### Protocols \n\nA protocol is a set of rules that define how communication occurs. \n\nProtocols specify: \n\n- Message format \n\n- Transmission methods \n\n- Error handling \n\n- Interpretation of information \n\n \n\n### Everyday Analogy \n\nHuman communication relies on rules. \n\nFor example: \n\n```text\n     One person speaks. \n     The other listens. \n```\n\nComputer communication also requires agreed-upon procedures. ","output":"","result":"## Communication Requires Rules \n\nImagine a conversation where everyone uses different languages. \n\nCommunication would be difficult. \n\nComputers face the same challenge. \n\nThey need common rules. \n\n### Protocols \n\nA protocol is a set of rules that define how communication occurs. \n\nProtocols specify: \n\n- Message format \n\n- Transmission methods \n\n- Error handling \n\n- Interpretation of information \n\n \n\n### Everyday Analogy \n\nHuman communication relies on rules. \n\nFor example: \n\n```text\n     One person speaks. \n     The other listens. \n```\n\nComputer communication also requires agreed-upon procedures. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3474-communication-requires-rules\">\n<h1>Communication Requires Rules</h1>\n<p>Imagine a conversation where everyone uses different languages.</p>\n<p>Communication would be difficult.</p>\n<p>Computers face the same challenge.</p>\n<p>They need common rules.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3474-protocols\">\n<h1>Protocols</h1>\n<p>A protocol is a set of rules that define how communication occurs.</p>\n<p>Protocols specify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Message format</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Transmission methods</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Error handling</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Interpretation of information</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3474-everyday-analogy\">\n<h1>Everyday Analogy</h1>\n<p>Human communication relies on rules.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     One person speaks. \n     The other listens. </pre>\n<p>Computer communication also requires agreed-upon procedures.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Packets \n\nInformation sent across networks is divided into smaller pieces called packets. \n\n### Why Packets? \n\nImagine sending a large book. \n\nInstead of transporting the entire book as a single object, you divide it into pages. \n\nPackets work similarly. \n\n \n\n### Packet Contents \n\nA packet typically contains: \n\n```text\n     Source Address \n     Destination Address \n     Data \n     Control Information \n```\n\n### Benefits \n\nPackets: \n- Improve efficiency \n\n- Support sharing of network resources \n\n- Allow information to be retransmitted if lost ","output":"","result":"## Packets \n\nInformation sent across networks is divided into smaller pieces called packets. \n\n### Why Packets? \n\nImagine sending a large book. \n\nInstead of transporting the entire book as a single object, you divide it into pages. \n\nPackets work similarly. \n\n \n\n### Packet Contents \n\nA packet typically contains: \n\n```text\n     Source Address \n     Destination Address \n     Data \n     Control Information \n```\n\n### Benefits \n\nPackets: \n- Improve efficiency \n\n- Support sharing of network resources \n\n- Allow information to be retransmitted if lost ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3475-packets\">\n<h1>Packets</h1>\n<p>Information sent across networks is divided into smaller pieces called packets.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3475-why-packets\">\n<h1>Why Packets?</h1>\n<p>Imagine sending a large book.</p>\n<p>Instead of transporting the entire book as a single object, you divide it into pages.</p>\n<p>Packets work similarly.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3475-packet-contents\">\n<h1>Packet Contents</h1>\n<p>A packet typically contains:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Source Address \n     Destination Address \n     Data \n     Control Information </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3475-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Packets:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Improve efficiency</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Support sharing of network resources</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Allow information to be retransmitted if lost</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Addresses on the Internet \n\nFor communication to occur, devices need addresses. \n\n### Postal Analogy \n\nWhen sending a letter, you need: \n\n```text\n     Sender Address \n\n     Recipient Address \n```\n\nNetworks operate similarly. \n\n### IP Addresses \n\nEvery device connected to a network receives an IP address. \n\nIP stands for: \n\n```text\n     Internet Protocol \n```\n\n### Example \n\nAn IP address may look like: \n\n```text\n     192.168.1.10 \n```\n\nor \n\n```text\n     2001:db8::1 \n```\n\n### Purpose \n\nIP addresses identify devices and allow information to reach the correct destination. ","output":"","result":"## Addresses on the Internet \n\nFor communication to occur, devices need addresses. \n\n### Postal Analogy \n\nWhen sending a letter, you need: \n\n```text\n     Sender Address \n\n     Recipient Address \n```\n\nNetworks operate similarly. \n\n### IP Addresses \n\nEvery device connected to a network receives an IP address. \n\nIP stands for: \n\n```text\n     Internet Protocol \n```\n\n### Example \n\nAn IP address may look like: \n\n```text\n     192.168.1.10 \n```\n\nor \n\n```text\n     2001:db8::1 \n```\n\n### Purpose \n\nIP addresses identify devices and allow information to reach the correct destination. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3476-addresses-on-the-internet\">\n<h1>Addresses on the Internet</h1>\n<p>For communication to occur, devices need addresses.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3476-postal-analogy\">\n<h1>Postal Analogy</h1>\n<p>When sending a letter, you need:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Sender Address \n\n     Recipient Address </pre>\n<p>Networks operate similarly.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3476-ip-addresses\">\n<h1>IP Addresses</h1>\n<p>Every device connected to a network receives an IP address.</p>\n<p>IP stands for:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Internet Protocol </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3476-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>An IP address may look like:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     192.168.1.10 </pre>\n<p>or</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     2001:db8::1 </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3476-purpose\">\n<h1>Purpose</h1>\n<p>IP addresses identify devices and allow information to reach the correct destination.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Domain Names \n\nHumans are poor at memorizing numerical addresses. \n\nImagine remembering: \n\n```text\n     142.250.184.206 \n```\ninstead of: \n\n```text\n     google.com \n```\n\n### Domain Names \n\nDomain names provide human-friendly names for computers and services. \n\nExamples: \n\n```text\n     google.com \n     microsoft.com \n     wikipedia.org \n```\n\n### Benefits \n\nDomain names are: \n\n- Easier to remember \n\n- Easier to communicate \n\n- Easier to organize ","output":"","result":"## Domain Names \n\nHumans are poor at memorizing numerical addresses. \n\nImagine remembering: \n\n```text\n     142.250.184.206 \n```\ninstead of: \n\n```text\n     google.com \n```\n\n### Domain Names \n\nDomain names provide human-friendly names for computers and services. \n\nExamples: \n\n```text\n     google.com \n     microsoft.com \n     wikipedia.org \n```\n\n### Benefits \n\nDomain names are: \n\n- Easier to remember \n\n- Easier to communicate \n\n- Easier to organize ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3477-domain-names\">\n<h1>Domain Names</h1>\n<p>Humans are poor at memorizing numerical addresses.</p>\n<p>Imagine remembering:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     142.250.184.206 </pre>\n<p>instead of:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     google.com </pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3477-domain-names-2\">\n<h1>Domain Names</h1>\n<p>Domain names provide human-friendly names for computers and services.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     google.com \n     microsoft.com \n     wikipedia.org </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3477-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Domain names are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Easier to remember</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Easier to communicate</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Easier to organize</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"### DNS: The Internet's Phone Book \n\nDNS stands for: \n\n```text\n     Domain Name System \n```\n\nIts purpose is to translate domain names into IP addresses. \n\n### Example \n\nUser enters: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\nDNS returns: \n```text\n     93.184.216.34 \n```\n\nThe browser can then contact the correct server. \n\n### Analogy \n\nDNS is similar to a contact list on a phone. \n\nYou remember: \n\n```text\n     Ben \n```\n\nrather than: \n\n```text\n     +32 xxx xx xx xx \n```\n\nDNS performs the same function for computers. ","output":"","result":"### DNS: The Internet's Phone Book \n\nDNS stands for: \n\n```text\n     Domain Name System \n```\n\nIts purpose is to translate domain names into IP addresses. \n\n### Example \n\nUser enters: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\nDNS returns: \n```text\n     93.184.216.34 \n```\n\nThe browser can then contact the correct server. \n\n### Analogy \n\nDNS is similar to a contact list on a phone. \n\nYou remember: \n\n```text\n     Ben \n```\n\nrather than: \n\n```text\n     +32 xxx xx xx xx \n```\n\nDNS performs the same function for computers. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3478-dns-the-internets-phone-book\">\n<h1>DNS: The Internet’s Phone Book</h1>\n<p>DNS stands for:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Domain Name System </pre>\n<p>Its purpose is to translate domain names into IP addresses.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3478-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>User enters:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     www.example.com </pre>\n<p>DNS returns:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     93.184.216.34 </pre>\n<p>The browser can then contact the correct server.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3478-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>DNS is similar to a contact list on a phone.</p>\n<p>You remember:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Ben </pre>\n<p>rather than:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     +32 xxx xx xx xx </pre>\n<p>DNS performs the same function for computers.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Clients and Servers \n\nMost Internet services follow a client-server model. \n\n### Client \n\nA client requests a service. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Web browser \n\n- Mobile app \n\n- Email application \n\n### Server \n\nA server provides a service. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Website server \n\n- Email server \n\n- Video streaming server \n\n### Example \n\n```text\n      Browser \n      (Client) \nRequest ↓  ↑ Response\n      Website \n      (Server) \n```\n\n### Everyday Analogy \n\nRestaurant: \n\n```text\n     Customer = Client \n     Kitchen = Server \n```\n\nThe customer requests a service. \n\nThe kitchen provides it. ","output":"","result":"## Clients and Servers \n\nMost Internet services follow a client-server model. \n\n### Client \n\nA client requests a service. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Web browser \n\n- Mobile app \n\n- Email application \n\n### Server \n\nA server provides a service. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Website server \n\n- Email server \n\n- Video streaming server \n\n### Example \n\n```text\n      Browser \n      (Client) \nRequest ↓  ↑ Response\n      Website \n      (Server) \n```\n\n### Everyday Analogy \n\nRestaurant: \n\n```text\n     Customer = Client \n     Kitchen = Server \n```\n\nThe customer requests a service. \n\nThe kitchen provides it. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3479-clients-and-servers\">\n<h1>Clients and Servers</h1>\n<p>Most Internet services follow a client-server model.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3479-client\">\n<h1>Client</h1>\n<p>A client requests a service.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Web browser</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Mobile app</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Email application</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3479-server\">\n<h1>Server</h1>\n<p>A server provides a service.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Website server</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Email server</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Video streaming server</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3479-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">      Browser \n      (Client) \nRequest ↓  ↑ Response\n      Website \n      (Server) </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3479-everyday-analogy\">\n<h1>Everyday Analogy</h1>\n<p>Restaurant:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Customer = Client \n     Kitchen = Server </pre>\n<p>The customer requests a service.</p>\n<p>The kitchen provides it.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The Internet Protocol (IP) \n\nThe Internet Protocol is responsible for delivering packets between devices. \n\n \n\n### Responsibilities \n\nIP determines: \n\n- Source address \n\n- Destination address \n\n- Routing through networks \n\n \n\n### Important Observation \n\nIP attempts delivery. \n\nIt does not guarantee delivery. \n\nFor reliability, additional protocols are needed. ","output":"","result":"## The Internet Protocol (IP) \n\nThe Internet Protocol is responsible for delivering packets between devices. \n\n \n\n### Responsibilities \n\nIP determines: \n\n- Source address \n\n- Destination address \n\n- Routing through networks \n\n \n\n### Important Observation \n\nIP attempts delivery. \n\nIt does not guarantee delivery. \n\nFor reliability, additional protocols are needed. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3480-the-internet-protocol-ip\">\n<h1>The Internet Protocol (IP)</h1>\n<p>The Internet Protocol is responsible for delivering packets between devices.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3480-responsibilities\">\n<h1>Responsibilities</h1>\n<p>IP determines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Source address</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Destination address</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Routing through networks</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3480-important-observation\">\n<h1>Important Observation</h1>\n<p>IP attempts delivery.</p>\n<p>It does not guarantee delivery.</p>\n<p>For reliability, additional protocols are needed.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## TCP \n\nTCP stands for: \n\n```text\n     Transmission Control Protocol \n```\n\nTCP provides reliable communication. \n\n### Responsibilities \n\nTCP ensures: \n\n- Packets arrive \n\n- Packets arrive correctly \n\n- Packets are reassembled in proper order \n\n### Analogy \n\nImagine sending numbered pages. \n\nTCP checks: \n\n```text\n     Page 5 arrived \n\n     Page 6 arrived \n\n     Page 7 arrived \n```\n\nIf page 6 is missing, TCP requests it again. \n\n\n### Applications \n\nTCP is commonly used for: \n\n- Websites \n\n- Email \n\n- File transfers \n\nwhere reliability is important. ","output":"","result":"## TCP \n\nTCP stands for: \n\n```text\n     Transmission Control Protocol \n```\n\nTCP provides reliable communication. \n\n### Responsibilities \n\nTCP ensures: \n\n- Packets arrive \n\n- Packets arrive correctly \n\n- Packets are reassembled in proper order \n\n### Analogy \n\nImagine sending numbered pages. \n\nTCP checks: \n\n```text\n     Page 5 arrived \n\n     Page 6 arrived \n\n     Page 7 arrived \n```\n\nIf page 6 is missing, TCP requests it again. \n\n\n### Applications \n\nTCP is commonly used for: \n\n- Websites \n\n- Email \n\n- File transfers \n\nwhere reliability is important. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3481-tcp\">\n<h1>TCP</h1>\n<p>TCP stands for:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Transmission Control Protocol </pre>\n<p>TCP provides reliable communication.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3481-responsibilities\">\n<h1>Responsibilities</h1>\n<p>TCP ensures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Packets arrive</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Packets arrive correctly</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Packets are reassembled in proper order</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3481-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>Imagine sending numbered pages.</p>\n<p>TCP checks:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Page 5 arrived \n\n     Page 6 arrived \n\n     Page 7 arrived </pre>\n<p>If page 6 is missing, TCP requests it again.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3481-applications\">\n<h1>Applications</h1>\n<p>TCP is commonly used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Websites</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Email</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>File transfers</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>where reliability is important.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## UDP \n\nUDP stands for: \n\n```text\n     User Datagram Protocol \n```\n\nUDP is simpler than TCP. \n\n\n### Characteristics \n\nUDP: \n\n- Is faster \n\n- Uses less overhead \n\n- Does not guarantee delivery \n\n \n\n### Analogy \n\nUDP is like a live conversation. \n\nIf one word is missed: \n\n```text\n     Conversation continues \n```\n\nThe missing word is usually not requested again. \n\n \n\n### Applications \n\nUDP is often used for: \n\n- Video conferencing \n\n- Online gaming \n\n- Streaming \n\nwhere speed is more important than perfect reliability. ","output":"","result":"## UDP \n\nUDP stands for: \n\n```text\n     User Datagram Protocol \n```\n\nUDP is simpler than TCP. \n\n\n### Characteristics \n\nUDP: \n\n- Is faster \n\n- Uses less overhead \n\n- Does not guarantee delivery \n\n \n\n### Analogy \n\nUDP is like a live conversation. \n\nIf one word is missed: \n\n```text\n     Conversation continues \n```\n\nThe missing word is usually not requested again. \n\n \n\n### Applications \n\nUDP is often used for: \n\n- Video conferencing \n\n- Online gaming \n\n- Streaming \n\nwhere speed is more important than perfect reliability. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3482-udp\">\n<h1>UDP</h1>\n<p>UDP stands for:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     User Datagram Protocol </pre>\n<p>UDP is simpler than TCP.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3482-characteristics\">\n<h1>Characteristics</h1>\n<p>UDP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Is faster</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Uses less overhead</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Does not guarantee delivery</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3482-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>UDP is like a live conversation.</p>\n<p>If one word is missed:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Conversation continues </pre>\n<p>The missing word is usually not requested again.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3482-applications\">\n<h1>Applications</h1>\n<p>UDP is often used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Video conferencing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Online gaming</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Streaming</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>where speed is more important than perfect reliability.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Routing \n\nPackets may travel through many networks before reaching their destination. \n\n \n\n### Routers \n\nRouters are devices that forward packets from one network to another. \n\n \n\n### Example \n\n```text\n     Computer \n        ↓ \n     Home Router \n        ↓ \n     Internet Provider \n        ↓ \n     Several Routers \n        ↓ \n     Destination Server \n```\n\n### Analogy \n\nRouting is similar to road navigation. \n\nRoad signs guide vehicles. \n\nRouters guide packets. ","output":"","result":"## Routing \n\nPackets may travel through many networks before reaching their destination. \n\n \n\n### Routers \n\nRouters are devices that forward packets from one network to another. \n\n \n\n### Example \n\n```text\n     Computer \n        ↓ \n     Home Router \n        ↓ \n     Internet Provider \n        ↓ \n     Several Routers \n        ↓ \n     Destination Server \n```\n\n### Analogy \n\nRouting is similar to road navigation. \n\nRoad signs guide vehicles. \n\nRouters guide packets. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3483-routing\">\n<h1>Routing</h1>\n<p>Packets may travel through many networks before reaching their destination.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3483-routers\">\n<h1>Routers</h1>\n<p>Routers are devices that forward packets from one network to another.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3483-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Computer \n        ↓ \n     Home Router \n        ↓ \n     Internet Provider \n        ↓ \n     Several Routers \n        ↓ \n     Destination Server </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3483-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>Routing is similar to road navigation.</p>\n<p>Road signs guide vehicles.</p>\n<p>Routers guide packets.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The World Wide Web \n\nMany people confuse: \n\n```text\n     Internet \n```\n\nand \n```text\n     Web \n```\n\nThey are not the same thing. \n\n\n### Internet \n\nThe infrastructure. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Networks \n\n- Routers \n\n- Protocols \n\n### World Wide Web \n\nA service running on top of the Internet. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Websites \n\n- Web applications \n\n\n### Important Observation \n\nThe Web is part of the Internet. \n\nThe Internet is larger than the Web. ","output":"","result":"## The World Wide Web \n\nMany people confuse: \n\n```text\n     Internet \n```\n\nand \n```text\n     Web \n```\n\nThey are not the same thing. \n\n\n### Internet \n\nThe infrastructure. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Networks \n\n- Routers \n\n- Protocols \n\n### World Wide Web \n\nA service running on top of the Internet. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Websites \n\n- Web applications \n\n\n### Important Observation \n\nThe Web is part of the Internet. \n\nThe Internet is larger than the Web. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3484-the-world-wide-web\">\n<h1>The World Wide Web</h1>\n<p>Many people confuse:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Internet </pre>\n<p>and</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Web </pre>\n<p>They are not the same thing.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3484-internet\">\n<h1>Internet</h1>\n<p>The infrastructure.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Networks</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Routers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Protocols</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3484-world-wide-web\">\n<h1>World Wide Web</h1>\n<p>A service running on top of the Internet.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Websites</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Web applications</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3484-important-observation\">\n<h1>Important Observation</h1>\n<p>The Web is part of the Internet.</p>\n<p>The Internet is larger than the Web.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Web Browsers \n\nA browser enables users to access web content. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Chrome \n\n- Edge \n\n- Firefox \n\n- Safari \n\n \n\n### Browser Responsibilities \n\nThe browser: \n\n- Requests web pages \n\n- Receives information \n\n- Displays content \n\n \n\n### Examples of Content \n\n- Text \n\n- Images \n\n- Videos \n\n- Interactive applications","output":"","result":"## Web Browsers \n\nA browser enables users to access web content. \n\nExamples: \n\n- Chrome \n\n- Edge \n\n- Firefox \n\n- Safari \n\n \n\n### Browser Responsibilities \n\nThe browser: \n\n- Requests web pages \n\n- Receives information \n\n- Displays content \n\n \n\n### Examples of Content \n\n- Text \n\n- Images \n\n- Videos \n\n- Interactive applications","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3485-web-browsers\">\n<h1>Web Browsers</h1>\n<p>A browser enables users to access web content.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Chrome</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Edge</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Firefox</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Safari</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3485-browser-responsibilities\">\n<h1>Browser Responsibilities</h1>\n<p>The browser:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Requests web pages</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Receives information</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Displays content</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3485-examples-of-content\">\n<h1>Examples of Content</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Text</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Images</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Videos</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Interactive applications</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Web Servers \n\nA web server stores and provides web content. \n\n \n\n### Example \n\nWhen visiting: \n\n```text\n     www.university.edu \n```\n\nthe browser contacts a web server that provides: \n\n- Text \n\n- Images \n\n- Layout information \n\n \n\n### Response \n\nThe browser receives the information and displays the page. ","output":"","result":"## Web Servers \n\nA web server stores and provides web content. \n\n \n\n### Example \n\nWhen visiting: \n\n```text\n     www.university.edu \n```\n\nthe browser contacts a web server that provides: \n\n- Text \n\n- Images \n\n- Layout information \n\n \n\n### Response \n\nThe browser receives the information and displays the page. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3486-web-servers\">\n<h1>Web Servers</h1>\n<p>A web server stores and provides web content.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3486-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>When visiting:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     www.university.edu </pre>\n<p>the browser contacts a web server that provides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Text</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Images</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Layout information</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3486-response\">\n<h1>Response</h1>\n<p>The browser receives the information and displays the page.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Cloud Computing \n\nMany modern services no longer run entirely on local computers. \n\nInstead they operate in the cloud. \n\n \n\n### What Is the Cloud? \n\nThe cloud simply refers to remote computers that provide services over a network. \n\n \n\nExamples \n\n- OneDrive \n\n- Google Drive \n\n- Netflix \n\n- Microsoft 365 \n\n- Online AI systems \n\n \n\n### Benefits \n\nCloud services provide: \n\n- Accessibility \n\n- Scalability \n\n- Resource sharing \n\n- Centralized management \n\n \n\n### Everyday Example \n\nInstead of storing a file locally: \n\n```text\n     Laptop \n```\n\nyou store it on: \n\n```text\n     Remote Server \n```\n\nand access it from anywhere. ","output":"","result":"## Cloud Computing \n\nMany modern services no longer run entirely on local computers. \n\nInstead they operate in the cloud. \n\n \n\n### What Is the Cloud? \n\nThe cloud simply refers to remote computers that provide services over a network. \n\n \n\nExamples \n\n- OneDrive \n\n- Google Drive \n\n- Netflix \n\n- Microsoft 365 \n\n- Online AI systems \n\n \n\n### Benefits \n\nCloud services provide: \n\n- Accessibility \n\n- Scalability \n\n- Resource sharing \n\n- Centralized management \n\n \n\n### Everyday Example \n\nInstead of storing a file locally: \n\n```text\n     Laptop \n```\n\nyou store it on: \n\n```text\n     Remote Server \n```\n\nand access it from anywhere. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3487-cloud-computing\">\n<h1>Cloud Computing</h1>\n<p>Many modern services no longer run entirely on local computers.</p>\n<p>Instead they operate in the cloud.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3487-what-is-the-cloud\">\n<h1>What Is the Cloud?</h1>\n<p>The cloud simply refers to remote computers that provide services over a network.</p>\n<p>Examples</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>OneDrive</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Google Drive</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Netflix</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Microsoft 365</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Online AI systems</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3487-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Cloud services provide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Accessibility</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Scalability</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Resource sharing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Centralized management</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3487-everyday-example\">\n<h1>Everyday Example</h1>\n<p>Instead of storing a file locally:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Laptop </pre>\n<p>you store it on:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Remote Server </pre>\n<p>and access it from anywhere.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## A Journey Through the Internet \n\nLet's follow what happens when a student visits: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\n### Step 1: User Enters Address \n\nThe student types: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\ninto a browser. \n\n \n\n### Step 2: DNS Lookup \n\nDNS translates: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\ninto an IP address. \n\n \n\n### Step 3: Contact the Server \n\nThe browser sends a request. \n\n```text\n     Client → Server \n```\n\n### Step 4: Routing \n\nPackets travel through multiple routers. \n\n```text\n     Home Network \n     ↓ \n     Internet Provider \n     ↓ \n     Internet \n     ↓ \n     Web Server \n```\n\n### Step 5: Server Response \n\nThe web server sends: \n\n- HTML \n\n- Images \n\n- Scripts \n\nback to the browser. \n\n### Step 6: Browser Displays the Page \n\nThe browser interprets the information and displays the website. \n\n \n\n### Remark \n\nThis entire process often completes in less than a second. ","output":"","result":"## A Journey Through the Internet \n\nLet's follow what happens when a student visits: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\n### Step 1: User Enters Address \n\nThe student types: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\ninto a browser. \n\n \n\n### Step 2: DNS Lookup \n\nDNS translates: \n\n```text\n     www.example.com \n```\n\ninto an IP address. \n\n \n\n### Step 3: Contact the Server \n\nThe browser sends a request. \n\n```text\n     Client → Server \n```\n\n### Step 4: Routing \n\nPackets travel through multiple routers. \n\n```text\n     Home Network \n     ↓ \n     Internet Provider \n     ↓ \n     Internet \n     ↓ \n     Web Server \n```\n\n### Step 5: Server Response \n\nThe web server sends: \n\n- HTML \n\n- Images \n\n- Scripts \n\nback to the browser. \n\n### Step 6: Browser Displays the Page \n\nThe browser interprets the information and displays the website. \n\n \n\n### Remark \n\nThis entire process often completes in less than a second. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-a-journey-through-the-internet\">\n<h1>A Journey Through the Internet</h1>\n<p>Let’s follow what happens when a student visits:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     www.example.com </pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-step-1-user-enters-address\">\n<h1>Step 1: User Enters Address</h1>\n<p>The student types:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     www.example.com </pre>\n<p>into a browser.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-step-2-dns-lookup\">\n<h1>Step 2: DNS Lookup</h1>\n<p>DNS translates:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     www.example.com </pre>\n<p>into an IP address.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-step-3-contact-the-server\">\n<h1>Step 3: Contact the Server</h1>\n<p>The browser sends a request.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Client → Server </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-step-4-routing\">\n<h1>Step 4: Routing</h1>\n<p>Packets travel through multiple routers.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">     Home Network \n     ↓ \n     Internet Provider \n     ↓ \n     Internet \n     ↓ \n     Web Server </pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-step-5-server-response\">\n<h1>Step 5: Server Response</h1>\n<p>The web server sends:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>HTML</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Images</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Scripts</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>back to the browser.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-step-6-browser-displays-the-page\">\n<h1>Step 6: Browser Displays the Page</h1>\n<p>The browser interprets the information and displays the website.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3488-remark\">\n<h1>Remark</h1>\n<p>This entire process often completes in less than a second.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Other Internet Services \n\nThe Internet supports much more than websites. \n\n \n\n### Email \n\nAllows asynchronous communication. \n\n \n\n### Video Streaming \n\nExamples: \n\n- YouTube \n\n- Netflix \n\n \n\n### Voice and Video Communication \n\nExamples: \n\n- Teams \n\n- Zoom \n\n- WhatsApp \n\n \n\n### Online Gaming \n\nConnects players worldwide. \n\n \n\n### Artificial Intelligence Services \n\nMany AI applications operate on remote servers and are accessed through the Internet. ","output":"","result":"## Other Internet Services \n\nThe Internet supports much more than websites. \n\n \n\n### Email \n\nAllows asynchronous communication. \n\n \n\n### Video Streaming \n\nExamples: \n\n- YouTube \n\n- Netflix \n\n \n\n### Voice and Video Communication \n\nExamples: \n\n- Teams \n\n- Zoom \n\n- WhatsApp \n\n \n\n### Online Gaming \n\nConnects players worldwide. \n\n \n\n### Artificial Intelligence Services \n\nMany AI applications operate on remote servers and are accessed through the Internet. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3489-other-internet-services\">\n<h1>Other Internet Services</h1>\n<p>The Internet supports much more than websites.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3489-email\">\n<h1>Email</h1>\n<p>Allows asynchronous communication.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3489-video-streaming\">\n<h1>Video Streaming</h1>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>YouTube</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Netflix</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3489-voice-and-video-communication\">\n<h1>Voice and Video Communication</h1>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Teams</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Zoom</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>WhatsApp</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3489-online-gaming\">\n<h1>Online Gaming</h1>\n<p>Connects players worldwide.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3489-artificial-intelligence-services\">\n<h1>Artificial Intelligence Services</h1>\n<p>Many AI applications operate on remote servers and are accessed through the Internet.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"### Summary \n\nIn this chapter we learned: \n\n- Networks connect computers and other devices. \n\n- The Internet is a global network of networks. \n\n- Protocols define communication rules. \n\n- Information is transmitted using packets. \n\n- IP addresses identify devices. \n\n- DNS translates domain names into IP addresses. \n\n- The client-server model underlies many Internet services. \n\n- IP handles addressing and routing. \n\n- TCP provides reliable communication. \n\n- UDP prioritizes speed over reliability. \n\n- Routers forward packets across networks. \n\n- The World Wide Web is a service built on top of the Internet. \n\n- Browsers and servers cooperate to provide web content. \n\n- Cloud computing uses remote servers to provide services. \n\n- Visiting a website involves many coordinated steps. ","output":"","result":"### Summary \n\nIn this chapter we learned: \n\n- Networks connect computers and other devices. \n\n- The Internet is a global network of networks. \n\n- Protocols define communication rules. \n\n- Information is transmitted using packets. \n\n- IP addresses identify devices. \n\n- DNS translates domain names into IP addresses. \n\n- The client-server model underlies many Internet services. \n\n- IP handles addressing and routing. \n\n- TCP provides reliable communication. \n\n- UDP prioritizes speed over reliability. \n\n- Routers forward packets across networks. \n\n- The World Wide Web is a service built on top of the Internet. \n\n- Browsers and servers cooperate to provide web content. \n\n- Cloud computing uses remote servers to provide services. \n\n- Visiting a website involves many coordinated steps. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3490-summary\">\n<h1>Summary</h1>\n<p>In this chapter we learned:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Networks connect computers and other devices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Internet is a global network of networks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Protocols define communication rules.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Information is transmitted using packets.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>IP addresses identify devices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The client-server model underlies many Internet services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>IP handles addressing and routing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TCP provides reliable communication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>UDP prioritizes speed over reliability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Routers forward packets across networks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The World Wide Web is a service built on top of the Internet.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Browsers and servers cooperate to provide web content.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud computing uses remote servers to provide services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Visiting a website involves many coordinated steps.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Key Terms \n|Term | Definition| \n|:----|:----------|\n|Network|Collection of devices capable of communication|\n|LAN |Local Area Network |\n|Internet |Global network of interconnected networks |\n|Protocol |Rules governing communication |\n|Packet |Unit of data transmitted across a network |\n|IP Address |Numerical identifier of a device |\n|Domain Name |Human-readable Internet address |\n|DNS |System translating names into IP addresses |\n|Client |Device or application requesting a service |\n|Server |Device providing a service |\n|IP |Internet Protocol |\n|TCP |Reliable transport protocol |\n|UDP |Lightweight transport protocol |\n|Router |Device forwarding packets between networks |\n|Web Browser |Application used to access web content |\n|Web Server |Server providing websites |\n|Cloud Computing |Delivery of services from remote systems |\n\n","output":"","result":"## Key Terms \n|Term | Definition| \n|:----|:----------|\n|Network|Collection of devices capable of communication|\n|LAN |Local Area Network |\n|Internet |Global network of interconnected networks |\n|Protocol |Rules governing communication |\n|Packet |Unit of data transmitted across a network |\n|IP Address |Numerical identifier of a device |\n|Domain Name |Human-readable Internet address |\n|DNS |System translating names into IP addresses |\n|Client |Device or application requesting a service |\n|Server |Device providing a service |\n|IP |Internet Protocol |\n|TCP |Reliable transport protocol |\n|UDP |Lightweight transport protocol |\n|Router |Device forwarding packets between networks |\n|Web Browser |Application used to access web content |\n|Web Server |Server providing websites |\n|Cloud Computing |Delivery of services from remote systems |\n\n","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3491-key-terms\">\n<h1>Key Terms</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"left\">Term</th>\n<th class=\"left\">Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Network</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Collection of devices capable of communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">LAN</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Local Area Network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Internet</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Global network of interconnected networks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Protocol</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Rules governing communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Packet</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Unit of data transmitted across a network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">IP Address</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Numerical identifier of a device</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Domain Name</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Human-readable Internet address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">DNS</td>\n<td class=\"left\">System translating names into IP addresses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Client</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Device or application requesting a service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Server</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Device providing a service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">IP</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Internet Protocol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">TCP</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Reliable transport protocol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">UDP</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Lightweight transport protocol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Router</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Device forwarding packets between networks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Web Browser</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Application used to access web content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Web Server</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Server providing websites</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"left\">Cloud Computing</td>\n<td class=\"left\">Delivery of services from remote systems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Review Questions \n\n1. What is a computer network? \n\n2. What is the difference between a LAN and the Internet? \n\n3. Why do networks require protocols? \n\n4. What is a packet? \n\n5. Why are IP addresses necessary? \n\n6. What problem does DNS solve? \n\n7. What is the difference between a client and a server? \n\n8. What is the role of IP? \n\n9. Why does TCP exist if IP already provides communication? \n\n10. When might UDP be preferred over TCP? \n\n11. What does a router do? \n\n12. What is the difference between the Internet and the World Wide Web? \n\n13. What is cloud computing? \n\n14. Describe the complete journey of a web request from browser to server and back. ","output":"","result":"## Review Questions \n\n1. What is a computer network? \n\n2. What is the difference between a LAN and the Internet? \n\n3. Why do networks require protocols? \n\n4. What is a packet? \n\n5. Why are IP addresses necessary? \n\n6. What problem does DNS solve? \n\n7. What is the difference between a client and a server? \n\n8. What is the role of IP? \n\n9. Why does TCP exist if IP already provides communication? \n\n10. When might UDP be preferred over TCP? \n\n11. What does a router do? \n\n12. What is the difference between the Internet and the World Wide Web? \n\n13. What is cloud computing? \n\n14. Describe the complete journey of a web request from browser to server and back. ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture08-c3492-review-questions\">\n<h1>Review Questions</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>What is a computer network?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What is the difference between a LAN and the Internet?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Why do networks require protocols?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What is a packet?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Why are IP addresses necessary?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What problem does DNS solve?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What is the difference between a client and a server?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What is the role of IP?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Why does TCP exist if IP already provides communication?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When might UDP be preferred over TCP?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What does a router do?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What is the difference between the Internet and the World Wide Web?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What is cloud computing?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Describe the complete journey of a web request from browser to server and back.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]